' Chapter 2/3 (class-stream) — a SECOND independent subscriber to `Broadcaster`'s own `onChanged`
' stream field, entirely from class code (`ClassStreamDemo.thr`'s own script subscribes directly
' too — see that file). Proves multiple subscribers on the same class-instance stream all receive
' the same emission independently.
'
' `.subscribe(m.onBroadcastChanged)` — a bare bound-method-reference argument, no call parens — is
' DSL sugar the compiler lowers to `.subscribe({ target: m, action: "onBroadcastChanged" })`. This
' is the REQUIRED pattern here, not a style choice — see GRAMMAR.md's "`stream`" section and
' findings/streams.md: a Function value's own `m` binding does not reliably survive being stored in
' the stream's own subscriber list and invoked later once detached from a real SceneGraph node
' (confirmed live, real device), since a `.flsh` instance is a plain associative array with no
' SceneGraph identity at all. Never write the inline-anonymous-function form here. See chapter 3
' (`/bound-method-sugar`) for the raw `{ target, action }` descriptor this sugar lowers to, shown
' side by side with this same sugar form.
class Listener {
  constructor() {
    private received: string = "none yet"
    private receiveCount: integer = 0
  }

  public function subscribeTo(broadcaster: object) {
    broadcaster.onChanged.subscribe(m.onBroadcastChanged)
  }

  public function onBroadcastChanged(value: string) {
    m.received = value
    m.receiveCount = m.receiveCount + 1
  }

  public function lastReceived(): string {
    return m.received
  }

  public function receivedCount(): integer {
    return m.receiveCount
  }
}
